      "body": "\n\n## Overview\n\nReject gRPC `Sleep` requests with levels above 2 before they reach the\nengine. Previously, unlike the Python and Rust HTTP endpoints, the gRPC\nendpoint forwarded unsupported levels and could leave model buffers\nunrecoverable after wake-up.\n\n\n\n## Claims\n\n- Return gRPC `INVALID_ARGUMENT` for sleep levels above 2.\n- Preserve the existing default and valid levels 0, 1, and 2.\n- Reject invalid levels before acquiring the RL lock or calling the engine.\n- Add an in-process gRPC regression test that verifies invalid requests are\n  not forwarded while level 2 remains accepted.\n\n\n\n## Validation\n```\ncargo nextest run -p vllm-server \\\n  control_sleep_rejects_level_above_two_before_engine_call\n\ncargo nextest run -p vllm-server --all-features --locked  --no-fail-fast\n\ncargo clippy  -p vllm-server --all-targets --all-features  --locked -- -D warnings\n```\n\n\n\n## Details\n\nThe Python sleep endpoint constrains `level` to the range 0 through 2, and\nthe Rust HTTP endpoint performs the same validation. The gRPC handler only\napplied the default value and forwarded the result directly to the engine.\n\nAn unsupported value such as 3 reaches the GPU worker as a level greater\nthan or equal to 1. Model buffers are saved only for level 2, while weights\nare offloaded only for level 1. As a result, level 3 can discard resources\nwithout preserving all state needed by wake-up, allowing later inference to\nreturn incorrect output without a request-level error.\n\nThe validation is placed at the gRPC boundary owned by `vllm-server` and\nuses the same error message as the Rust HTTP endpoint. No protobuf, engine,\nworker, or Python behavior is changed.\n\n\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [ ] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [ ] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [ ] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [ ] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [ ] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n",
